WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.880
ai just built a driving simulator so realistic it feels like GTA without the game. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 235. A deep dive on how the pictures keep moving, a super Nintendo that chats, and a weekend review. Pan 2, which Linus Eckenstem posted on X, is a free open source world model. Open source means anyone can use it and inspect how it works. A world model is an ai that builds a whole scene, then predicts how that scene changes when you act.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.080
like turning the wheel or hitting the brakes.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.940
Think of it like a racing game that draws the next second of street after you turn the wheel.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.780
It is not loading a track someone already built.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.320
One clip shows driving through what feels like Miami, with handling on wet surfaces that

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.380
matches real streets.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.580
Another shows a driver actually waiting at a red light, something most video games skip

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.880
so the action never stops.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:58.620
A police car chases a motorbike down a narrow wet back alley, then smashes into a stack

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.720
of boxes. The boxes tumbled the way you would expect in real life. There are clips of

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.300
driving straight into the ocean too, and what happens next looks surprisingly natural.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.820
For anyone who loves games or wants to make stories and videos, this is AI as a creative

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:19.500
playground, not just a homework helper. Later on, the same kind of tool could let you build game

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.880
levels or test ideas without expensive software or coding skills. Pan 2 runs locally on capable

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:30.740
hardware, meaning on the computer in front of you. People can pause a scene and change

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:36.320
the starting conditions. They immediately see how a small tweak changes the outcome. Developers

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.240
are already sharing ways to try similar experiments as they become available. You can search for

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.840
the posts from Linus Eckenstam to watch the red light scene and the chaotic chase yourself.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:50.700
Your brain already guesses the landing when a skateboarder leaves a ramp in a YouTube clip.

00:01:51.604 --> 00:01:56.104
World models do a similar kind of guessing, except they work with pictures instead of

00:01:56.104 --> 00:01:56.504
words.

00:01:56.904 --> 00:02:00.964
They look at the current scene and break it into tiny pieces of information about where

00:02:00.964 --> 00:02:03.424
everything is and how fast things are moving.

00:02:03.944 --> 00:02:08.464
Then they predict the most likely next frame, meaning the next still picture in the sequence.

00:02:09.184 --> 00:02:13.524
First, the AI studies thousands of real driving videos and learns basic rules.

00:02:14.104 --> 00:02:17.584
Cars usually stop at red lights and wet roads make things slide.

00:02:18.224 --> 00:02:20.004
Next you give it a new starting scene.

00:02:20.504 --> 00:02:24.864
It generates what comes after by checking those learned rules against the exact positions

00:02:24.864 --> 00:02:26.224
and speeds in that moment.

00:02:26.824 --> 00:02:30.924
If something unexpected happens like a sudden crash, it adjusts the prediction on the

00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:31.224
fly.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:36.324
It uses the same pattern matching, looking for the closest match to situations it has seen.

00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:41.464
It does not store every possible outcome in advance, and it is not copying old footage.

00:02:42.024 --> 00:02:46.064
It rebuilds each new frame from scratch using the patterns it has seen before.

00:02:46.704 --> 00:02:51.044
That is why the boxes fall differently every time, depending on exactly where the car hits

00:02:51.044 --> 00:02:51.264
them.

00:02:51.264 --> 00:02:56.724
Smooth, believable motion is just that guess, rebuilt again and again from the rules.

00:02:57.244 --> 00:03:01.904
So the trick has a plain name now, it is next frame guessing, and you have been doing

00:03:01.904 --> 00:03:04.004
a version of it every time you watch a clip.

00:03:04.604 --> 00:03:10.104
The original Super Nintendo from 1990, Tom's hardware reports, just got an upgrade that

00:03:10.104 --> 00:03:12.664
lets it run a tiny version of chat GPT.

00:03:13.324 --> 00:03:18.364
Researchers used a special add-on chip called the Super FX2 to speed the old hardware enough

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.904
for simple AI conversations.

00:03:19.904 --> 00:03:24.264
The chip runs at 21 MHz, a measure of how fast it ticks.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:29.724
It uses risk, a stripped-down chip design that does fewer kinds of jobs but does them quickly.

00:03:30.404 --> 00:03:33.524
That is enough to run a 2.2 million parameter chatbot.

00:03:34.164 --> 00:03:38.184
Parameters are the little settings that shape its answers, and this tiny model is still

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:39.744
fast enough for real replies.

00:03:40.344 --> 00:03:45.244
It shows how far AI has come, that a 35-year-old game system can chat with you.

00:03:45.244 --> 00:03:49.804
Smaller AI models can run on very limited hardware when they get the right boost.

00:03:50.284 --> 00:03:54.644
People can experiment on devices they already own instead of needing the newest computers.

00:03:55.244 --> 00:04:00.364
Retro gaming fans and anyone curious about old tech get a mashup of 1990's hardware

00:04:00.364 --> 00:04:01.764
in 2020's AI.

00:04:02.364 --> 00:04:05.504
Search Tom's hardware for Super Nintendo Chat GPT.

00:04:06.044 --> 00:04:10.504
Then play the embedded clip to hear the old console answer basic questions in real time.

00:04:10.504 --> 00:04:17.019
Some schools in the UK, the Daily Mail Reports, are now officially allowing teachers to use AI chat

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:22.559
bots when grading student coursework, even as critics call the move scandalous. Officials gave

00:04:22.559 --> 00:04:27.279
the permission quietly, which has sparked debate over whether parents and students should be told

00:04:27.279 --> 00:04:32.819
more openly. The change could speed up feedback, and it raises questions about how much human judgment

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:39.484
should stay in the process. Top forecasters and mathematician Terence Tau, in posts on X,

00:04:39.484 --> 00:04:44.084
guests last month, AI would need years or decades to help solve the Riemann hypothesis.

00:04:44.744 --> 00:04:49.924
That is one of Matt's biggest open problems, a famous unsolved question about the hidden

00:04:49.924 --> 00:04:51.224
pattern in prime numbers.

00:04:51.764 --> 00:04:59.084
A forecasting group called Metacallulus predicted May 2035, a team called Leap Super Forecasters

00:04:59.084 --> 00:05:05.044
gave only a 10% chance of another millennium problem, one of the famous unsolved prize puzzles

00:05:05.044 --> 00:05:08.004
in math, being solved in 2026.

00:05:08.004 --> 00:05:13.104
Tau suggested some combination of humans and AIS around 2050.

00:05:13.764 --> 00:05:16.824
New progress has already made those timelines look too slow.

